    dcterms:description "The Pincian Hill in Rome likely takes its name from the Pincii, an ancient family. It is not considered a member of the canonical grouping of Rome's hills, but it did lie within the line of the walls built by Aurelian. In the early Imperial period, it is known as a zone of elaborate gardens, hence the moniker \"collis hortulorum\".";
    dcterms:modified "2025-11-28T20:08:36-04:00";
    dcterms:title "Pincian Hill";
