<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/801400483> a <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/vocab#Place>;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "EDH HD023604",
        "James 2019a pages 109-118",
        "Wikidata Bath F3, Dura-Europos (Q121301893)";
    dcterms:contributor <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/jbecker>;
    dcterms:creator <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/achen>;
    dcterms:description "Bath building originally occupying all of Block F3, located in the intramuros military sector of Dura-Europos. Excavations reveal that the bath likely had two phases of renovation (one perhaps after 165 CE, and another around c. 200 CE), before it was destroyed by fire. Dura-Europos' amphitheater was built over the remains of Bath F3, and thus the amphitheater's dedicatory inscription, dated to 216 CE, serves as a terminus ante quem for the bath building.";
    dcterms:modified "2025-02-06T00:39:09-04:00";
    dcterms:subject "YDEA project",
        "baths";
    dcterms:title "Bath F3";
    cito:citesAsEvidence <http://edh-www.adw.uni-heidelberg.de/HD023604>;
    cito:citesAsRelated <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q121301893>;
    cito:citesForInformation <https://www.worldcat.org/title/1369556517>;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/181763748>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/356665990>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/893990>;
    owl:sameAs <http://pleiades.stoa.org/places/801400483>;
    skos:inScheme <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places>;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom [ rdfs:label "Pleiades" ];
    pleiades:hasFeatureType <https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/bath> .

<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/802040030%23this> a <http://geovocab.org/spatial#Feature>;
    rdfs:label "Rotes Haus (Tell Schech Hamad)";
    spatial:C <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/863979041%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/893991%23this>;
    rdfs:comment "The so-called \"Red House\" is a large building at Dūr-Katlimmu that was built and occupied between the sixth and fifth centuries BCE. The structure included administrative, reception, and living areas, and a few Assyrian cuneiform texts were recovered during its excavation.";
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/802040030> .

