<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/846526213%23this> a <http://geovocab.org/spatial#Feature>;
    rdfs:label "Kidmuri temple (Kalhu)";
    spatial:C <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/439136375%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/62810658%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/894019%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/897364594%23this>;
    rdfs:comment "When Kalhu (biblical Calah, modern Nimrud) became the principal administrative center of the Assyrian Empire in the ninth century BC, king Ashurnasirpal II (r. 883–859 BC) built (or rebuilt) numerous temples. One of those was dedicated to the divine Kidmuri (probably a collective name for a group of gods).";
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/846526213> .

