<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/889344378> a <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/vocab#Place>;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "ATAE Kalhu 14 ",
        "Pedersén 1998 151–152";
    dcterms:contributor <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/jnovotny>;
    dcterms:creator <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/tseidler>;
    dcterms:description "The remains of a library containing about 280 clay tablets were found in and around Room NT12 the Nabû Temple (Ezida). These text date from 800 BC to 614 BC. The majority of the texts assigned to this archive repository were discovered in Room NT12 and the adjoining courtyard. Some texts, including fragments of seven wooden and ivory writing boards, were found in Rooms NT13 and NT14. This Neo-Assyrian ‘library’ house omen texts (79 tablets), incantation and medical texts (75 tablets), lexical lists (37 tablets), as well as some administrative texts, epics, hemerological texts, hymns, prayers, rituals, and royal inscriptions.";
    dcterms:modified "2023-05-11T12:48:50-04:00";
    dcterms:subject "ANE",
        "Assyrian";
    dcterms:title "Kalhu Archive 14";
    cito:citesForInformation <http://oracc.museum.upenn.edu/atae/kalhu_014>,
        <http://uu.diva-portal.org/smash/get/diva2:88568/FULLTEXT01.pdf>;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/242008148>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/894019>;
    owl:sameAs <http://pleiades.stoa.org/places/889344378>;
    skos:altLabel "Kalhu Archive 14"@en;
    skos:inScheme <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places>;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom [ rdfs:label "Pleiades" ];
    pleiades:hasFeatureType <https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/archive-repository>;
    pleiades:hasLocation <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/plan-location-of-kalhu-archive-14>;
    pleiades:hasName <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/kalhu-archive-14> .

<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/889344379> a <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/vocab#Place>;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Pedersén 1998 152";
    dcterms:contributor <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/jnovotny>;
    dcterms:creator <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/tseidler>;
    dcterms:description "British excavators found fragments of at least eight or nine large clay tablets in the Throne Room of the Nabû Temple (Ezida). These now-fragmentarily-preserved tablets, all of which date to 672 BC, contain copies of treaties that seventh-century-BC Assyrian king Esarhaddon (r. 680–669 BC) made with several Median client rulers. These ‘covenant’ documents — which are often referred to as “Esarhaddon’s Succession Treaty” or “Esarhaddon’s Vassal Treaty” — were written up to ensure loyalty to Ashurbanipal, Esarhaddon’s fourth eldest son, when he ascended the Assyrian throne upon the death of his father.";
    dcterms:modified "2022-10-10T09:31:10-04:00";
    dcterms:subject "ANE",
        "Assyrian";
    dcterms:title "Kalhu Archive 15";
    cito:citesForInformation <http://uu.diva-portal.org/smash/get/diva2:88568/FULLTEXT01.pdf>;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/242008148>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/894019>;
    owl:sameAs <http://pleiades.stoa.org/places/889344379>;
    skos:altLabel "Kalhu Archive 15"@en;
    skos:inScheme <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places>;
