<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/897225060%23this> a <http://geovocab.org/spatial#Feature>;
    rdfs:label "Nēmed-Enlil";
    spatial:C <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/202833933%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/223617668%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/223954669%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/386554382%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/438874660%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/452038953%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/541602250%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/632147764%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/653377582%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/704495840%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/794488651%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/893951%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/897449051%23this>;
    rdfs:comment "The outer wall of Babylon was called by the ceremonial Akkadian name Nēmed-Enlil/ Nēmetti-Enlil, which means \"Bulwark of the God Enlil.\" According to the text Tintir = Babylon, Nēmed-Enlil (and the inner wall Imgur-Enlil) had eight city gates, four on the eastern side of the Euphrates and four on the western side of the river. The Assyrian kings Esarhaddon and Ashurbanipal and the Babylonian kings Nabopolassar, Nebuchadnezzar II, and Nebonidus all undertook construction on Babylon’s outer wall. ";
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/897225060> .

<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/897364594%23this> a <http://geovocab.org/spatial#Feature>;
    rdfs:label "Gula temple (Kalhu)";
    spatial:C <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/344586145%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/439136375%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/62810658%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/846526213%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/894019%23this>;
    rdfs:comment "When Kalhu (biblical Calah, modern Nimrud) became the principal administrative center of the Assyrian Empire in the ninth century BC, king Ashurnasirpal II (r. 883–859 BC) built (or rebuilt) numerous temples. One of those was dedicated to the goddess Gula. Although its location is not known, the Gula temple at Kalhu is thought to have been part of the Ninurta temple complex (in the northwest corner of the citadel), east of the Šarrat-niphi temple and north of the Kidmuri temple.";
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/897364594> .

<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/897449051%23this> a <http://geovocab.org/spatial#Feature>;
    rdfs:label "King's Gate";
    spatial:C <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/202833933%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/633091638%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/893951%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/897225060%23this>;
    rdfs:comment "The King's Gate is one of Babylon's eight city gates and it is situated on the western side of the inner city, directly west of the nu-x-ù quarter. King Nebuchadnezzar II built the gate ca. 575 B.C.";
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/897449051> .

